@startuml chomsky-placement
' D · Placement of duallity's WFSTs in the Chomsky hierarchy.
' Strictly nested rings: Type 0 ⊋ Type 1 ⊋ Type 2 ⊋ Type 3. Levenshtein / phonetic WFSTs live
' inside the innermost Type-3 (regular) box; {a^n b^n} sits in the Type-2 ring, OUTSIDE Type 3.
skinparam backgroundColor #FFFFFF
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam defaultFontName "DejaVu Sans"
skinparam rectangle {
  BorderColor #34495E
  FontName "DejaVu Sans"
}
title Chomsky hierarchy — where duallity's automata sit\nnesting arrow <latex>\supseteq</latex> means "properly contains" (<latex>\subseteq</latex> strict)

rectangle "Type 0 — Recursively enumerable (RE)\nunrestricted grammars · Turing machines" as T0 #FBFCFC {
  rectangle "Type 1 — Context-sensitive (CS)\nnon-contracting grammars · linear-bounded automata" as T1 #F2F4F4 {
    rectangle "Type 2 — Context-free (CF)\nCF grammars · pushdown automata" as T2 #E5E8E8 {

      rectangle "<latex>\{\, a^{n} b^{n} : n \ge 0 \,\}</latex>\nnot regular  (pumping lemma)\nneeds a stack to count" as ANBN #E8DAEF

      rectangle "Type 3 — Regular\nfinite automata · regular expressions" as T3 #D5D8DC {
        rectangle "Levenshtein / phonetic WFSTs\n(duallity)\nfinite states · linear-time recognition" as WFST #D6EAF8
      }
    }
  }
}

ANBN -[hidden]down- T3

note bottom
  duallity's Levenshtein and phonetic transducers are <b>weighted finite-state</b> machines:
  finitely many states, no unbounded memory, so they recognize exactly the <b>Type-3 (regular)</b>
  languages and run in time linear in the query length. The classic non-regular witness
  <latex>\{a^{n} b^{n}\}</latex> lies one ring out, in <b>Type 2</b>: counting <latex>n</latex> unbounded <latex>a</latex>'s then matching
  <latex>n</latex> <latex>b</latex>'s requires a stack, which a finite-state machine does not have.
end note
@enduml
